<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; Chapter 3 &gt; Level 3</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 3 Level 3 Walkthrough: Study Room</h1>

    <p>The Study Room is room 3 of Chapter 3, <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>, and it is the chapter's computer level: a laptop that takes an eight-digit password, then a five-digit verification code, then hands you a five-digit code for a keypad on the wall. There is also a hidden rotary safe behind a hinged panel, and a book full of economics that looks like a cipher key and is not. This page gives you <b>20062015</b>, <b>45837</b> and <b>62850</b>, shows where each one comes from, and tells you which props are dead ends so you stop reading them.</p>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Built from one 5:13 recording, one frame per second (313 frames). Every character printed here was read twice: once from the full frame and once from a 3x to 5x crop, and the three codes were additionally cross-checked with macOS Vision OCR over all 313 frames. Where a value could only be read from a single source, it is listed under <i>What this page could not verify</i> instead of being presented as fact.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={3} current={3} variant="jump" label="Chapter 3 · Murder Dressed in White" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>Study Room is room 3 of 15 in Chapter 3. Four locks, three of them in one chain:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Laptop password: 20062015</b> — read the <b>Photograph</b> in your inventory; it is dated <b>20.06.2015</b>.</li>
        <li><b>2-step verification: 45837</b> — five digits, shown in the message bubble on the <b>phone</b> on the desk.</li>
        <li><b>Final keypad: 62850</b> — the <b>laptop displays it</b> once you are logged in, then you type it on the five-digit keypad.</li>
        <li><b>Hidden wall safe: 30 anti-clockwise, 40 clockwise, 55 anti-clockwise, 70 clockwise</b> — the game's own hint text, and the dial the player works at 2:51.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The order matters: password → verification → keypad. The safe is a side branch and does not feed the chain.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
      <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="2S33pol9jGE" data-title="Rooms &amp; Exits Level 3 Study Room Walkthrough Murder Dressed in White Chapter 3"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp; Exits Level 3 Study Room Walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/2S33pol9jGE/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 3 Study Room walkthrough video" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/2S33pol9jGE/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="video-cap">The 5:13 run used for every frame on this page.</p>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-hero-study-room.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 3 Level 3 Study Room: dark wood desk and black office chair, tall bookshelves, a lounge chair with a blue throw, a marble side table with a decanter, and herringbone parquet flooring" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>A gentleman's study, and the richest room in the chapter visually: dark panelled walls, herringbone parquet, a heavy wooden desk with a black office chair, floor-to-ceiling bookshelves, a lit fireplace with a mantel, a lounge chair with a blue throw, and a marble-topped side table carrying a decanter, an ice bucket, a glass of amber liquid and a bowl of snacks.</p>
    <p>The puzzle structure is a single linear chain plus one side branch. The chain runs <b>Photograph → laptop password → 2-step verification → laptop reveals a code → keypad</b>, and it is genuinely linear: you cannot skip the verification, and the keypad code is not written anywhere in the room — the laptop prints it for you. The side branch is the <b>rotary wall safe</b> behind the hinged panel, which takes a four-turn dial combination and sits outside the main chain.</p>
    <p>Everything else is noise, and there is a lot of it: five inventory items with no lock to use them on, an eleven-entry economics list, a shelf of books with Roman-numeral hints, and a glowing round mechanism that never pays off. The recording spends most of its middle three minutes on that noise.</p>

    <h2>The map: what is where<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <table class="item-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Object</th><th>Where</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Laptop (Acer)</td><td>On the desk</td><td>Password <b>20062015</b>, then 2-step <b>45837</b>, then displays <b>62850</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Photograph</td><td>Carried item</td><td>Dated <b>20.06.2015</b> — the source of the laptop password.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Phone</td><td>On the desk</td><td>Message bubble reads <b>45837</b> — the 2-step verification code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Keypad</td><td>On the wall / panel</td><td>Five-digit pad with 0–9, X and an enter arrow. Takes <b>62850</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hidden safe</td><td>Behind a hinged white panel, right of the fireplace</td><td>Rotary dial safe. <b>30 CCW, 40 CW, 55 CCW, 70 CW</b>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Hinged panel</td><td>Wood-panelled wall</td><td>Already open at 0:54; hides the safe. Wall sconce to its right.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Round glowing mechanism</td><td>Dark wood wall</td><td>White square core. Touched at 0:44, never explained.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Economics book</td><td>On the desk</td><td>Printed list of eleven economic indicators. Looks like a cipher key; unused.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Bookshelves</td><td>Back wall</td><td><b>RUDYARD KIPLING</b>, <b>JUNGLE BOOK</b>, <b>BOLTON</b>, <b>BERTHA RUNKLE</b>. Roman-numeral hint: IV=4, III=3, VI=7.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Side table</td><td>Beside the lounge chair</td><td>Decanter, ice bucket, glass, snacks. Scenery with a close-up at 0:05.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Blanket / Shovel / Brush / Ash / Light bulb</td><td>Carried items</td><td>All picked up in the run, none used on a lock.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fingerprint</td><td>Carried item</td><td>Produced by the Brush at 1:41; no lock consumes it.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Order follows the recording. Timestamps are video time; the links jump to the matching second.</p>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:02</span>
      <strong>Sweep the study</strong>
      The level opens on the chapter map, then cuts to the room. Desk and chair centre, bookshelves behind, fireplace and lounge to the right, side table with the decanter at the edge. The two things you are looking for are both on the desk: the <b>laptop</b> and the <b>phone</b>. Everything else is scenery until the safe shows up.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-hero-study-room.jpg" alt="The Study Room at the start of Chapter 3 Level 3, showing the desk, chair, bookshelves and lounge" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=2s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:02</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:29</span>
      <strong>Fireplace, mantel, desk lamp</strong>
      Panning to the fireplace side gives you the room's second half: a lit fire, a mantel with a small plant, more bookshelves, the desk lamp. No lock here — but this is where the <b>hinged panel</b> and the <b>wall sconce</b> live, and they are easy to miss if you only ever look at the desk.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-desk-and-fireplace.jpg" alt="The fireplace, mantel and desk area of the Study Room" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=29s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:29</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:44 · the odd one</span>
      <strong>A round mechanism with a glowing square</strong>
      A dark grey circular fitting set into the panelling with a bright white square in the middle. The player taps it once, early, and it never comes back. It is not the safe, and it is not a keypad — if you spend ten minutes trying to make it do something, you are in good company.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=44s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:44</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 0:54 · the safe</span>
      <strong>The hinged panel hides a rotary safe</strong>
      A white-framed panel on the panelled wall swings open to show a dark grey safe with a dial, next to a glowing wall sconce. This is the only rotary lock in the room, and it is the one that takes four turns rather than four digits. Its combination is covered at step 8.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-hidden-safe-dial.jpg" alt="The hidden wall safe in the Study Room, revealed behind an open hinged panel beside a wall sconce" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=54s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:54</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 1:07–1:41 · the bag fills up</span>
      <strong>Blanket, Shovel, Ash, Brush, Fingerprint</strong>
      The player collects a run of items: <b>Blanket</b>, <b>Shovel</b>, <b>Ash</b>, <b>Brush</b>, and a <b>Light bulb</b> later at 3:11. Using the Brush produces a <b>Fingerprint</b> at 1:41. All of them sit in the bag with Use / Combine / Dismantle / Examine and none of them is consumed by a lock in this run — so this is optional content, not a required step.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=67s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:07</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:51 · the economics book</span>
      <strong>The eleven-item list is a dead end</strong>
      A thick book opens on a printed page: <i>The economy may be considered as having developed through the following phases or degrees of precedence</i>, then a numbered list — 1 Consumer spending, 2 Exchange rate, 3 Gross domestic product, 4 GDP per capita, 5 GNP, 6 Stock market, 7 Interest rate, 8 Government debt, 9 Rate of inflation, 10 Unemployment, 11 Balance of trade. It reads exactly like the key to an eleven-symbol cipher. Nothing in the level ever uses it.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=111s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:51</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 2:28 · the photograph</span>
      <strong>Photograph: 20.06.2015</strong>
      Open the bag and there it is — a small <b>Photograph</b> with the date printed on it: <b>20.06.2015</b>. This is the level's best clue because it is the only one that tells you how to build a password: drop the dots and you have eight digits in a row. Do not read it as 20-06-2015 with separators; the laptop wants the digits run together.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-photograph-20062015.jpg" alt="The Photograph inventory item in the Study Room, dated 20.06.2015" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=148s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:28</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 2:04 · the safe combination</span>
      <strong>30 anticlockwise, 40 clockwise, 55 anticlockwise, 70 clockwise</strong>
      The game's hint for this level spells the safe out as four numbered moves: <i>1. 30 anti clockwise / 2. 40 clockwise / 3. 55 anti clockwise / 4. 70 clockwise</i>. Note that it alternates direction every turn, and that the numbers are dial positions, not clicks. The player works the dial between <b>2:51</b> and <b>3:05</b>, where the dial face shows the 20 / 30 / 60 / 70 / 90 marks.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-hint-rotary-dial.jpg" alt="The in-game hint text listing the Study Room safe combination: 30 anti clockwise, 40 clockwise, 55 anti clockwise, 70 clockwise" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=124s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:04</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:35 · the login screen</span>
      <strong>PASWORD, and a field that wants eight digits</strong>
      The laptop shows a login card headed <b>PASWORD</b> — the game's own spelling, missing the S — with a text field and a <b>Sign in</b> button, plus the on-screen keyboard. This is the first real lock of the chain and it wants the date from the Photograph.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=155s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:35</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:40 · type the password</span>
      <strong>20062015</strong>
      Eight digits, typed in one run: <b>2 0 0 6 2 0 1 5</b>. You can watch it build frame by frame — 20, then 20062, then 2006201, then the full 20062015 — which is exactly why we are confident in every digit. Then <b>Sign in</b>.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-laptop-password.jpg" alt="The Study Room laptop password field with 20062015 typed in" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=160s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:40</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 2:45 · the wall</span>
      <strong>2-Step Verification wants five more digits</strong>
      The password is accepted and the laptop immediately blocks you again: <b>2-Step Verification / ENTER YOUR VERIFICATION CODE</b>, with a fresh field and Verify button. At this point in the recording the player has no code — this is where the level sends you back into the room to find the phone.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=165s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:45</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 12 · 4:22 · the phone</span>
      <strong>Message bubble: 45837</strong>
      Pick up the phone on the desk. The screen shows a search bar at the top and one grey message bubble in the middle with five digits in it: <b>45837</b>. No unlock, no second message, no scrolling. That is the verification code.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-phone-message-45837.jpg" alt="The phone in the Study Room showing a message bubble with the code 45837" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=262s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:22</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 13 · 4:41 · verify</span>
      <strong>Type 45837 and verify</strong>
      Back to the laptop, into the verification field, five digits: <b>4 5 8 3 7</b>. Again the build-up is visible frame by frame — 45, then 458, then 4583, then 45837. Two different screens show the same five digits, which is why this one is printed without a caveat.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-two-step-code.jpg" alt="The 2-Step Verification screen on the Study Room laptop with 45837 entered" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=281s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:41</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 14 · 4:46 · the payoff</span>
      <strong>The laptop prints 62850</strong>
      Verification clears and the laptop opens on a screen with a light panel showing <b>62850</b> — five digits, no label, no explanation. This is the keypad code. It is the only place in the room where it appears, so if you skip the laptop you will not find it written anywhere else.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-laptop-code-62850.jpg" alt="The unlocked Study Room laptop display showing the code 62850" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2S33pol9jGE&t=286s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">4:46</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 15 · 5:08 · the keypad</span>
      <strong>62850 on the five-digit pad</strong>
      The keypad is a clean 0–9 pad with an X and an enter arrow, and the field above it already reads <b>62850</b>. No dial, no letters, no length guessing — this pad is five digits and the laptop just told you which five. Entering it is the last action in the recording.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-final-keypad-62850.jpg" alt="The five-digit keypad in the Study Room with 62850 entered" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
