    <h2>Books and numerals: what the shelf is for<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The bookshelves get a lot of screen time and the readable spines are <b>RUDYARD KIPLING</b>, <b>JUNGLE BOOK</b>, <b>BOLTON</b> and <b>BERTHA RUNKLE</b>, plus one stamped spine number that OCRs as <b>1745</b> but shifts between frames. The game's own hint for this shelf says: <i>Look at the bookshelves with the Globe on, notice the blue books and how they stand — IV = 4, III = 3, VI = 7.</i> So the shelf is a counting puzzle: blue books standing in Roman numerals, read as digits. It is not a printed code and it does not feed the laptop.</p>
    <p>That hint also tells you something useful about the level's design: when a shelf looks like a cipher, the game will eventually say so in a hint, and the hint will be about <i>how the books stand</i> rather than what is printed on them. If you are stuck on the books, count the blue spines rather than reading titles.</p>
    <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c3l3-bookshelf-books.jpg" alt="The Study Room bookshelves with readable spines including Rudyard Kipling, Jungle Book, Bolton and Bertha Runkle" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p><b>"The laptop password does not work."</b> It is <b>20062015</b>, eight digits, no dots, no slashes. The Photograph prints it as 20.06.2015 and the two most common mistakes are keeping the separators and typing 20062015 with a leading zero dropped — there is no leading zero, but there are two zeros in the middle: 2-0-0-6-2-0-1-5.</p>
    <p><b>"I am past the password but stuck on verification."</b> The code is not in the laptop, the books or the safe. It is on the <b>phone</b> on the desk: <b>45837</b>. If your phone shows nothing, you have not picked it up yet — it is a carried item, not a wall object.</p>
    <p><b>"Where is the code for the keypad?"</b> The laptop prints <b>62850</b> only after verification. Players hunt the room for a five-digit number on a wall and there is not one; the laptop is the only source.</p>
    <p><b>"The safe dial does nothing."</b> It takes four alternating turns, not four digits: <b>30 anti-clockwise, 40 clockwise, 55 anti-clockwise, 70 clockwise</b>. Turn anticlockwise first — starting clockwise resets the dial, which is why people report that "the first number will not take".</p>
    <p><b>"What do I do with the Blanket, Shovel, Brush, Ash and Light bulb?"</b> Nothing, in this run. They are collected and never consumed. The same goes for the Fingerprint the Brush produces and for the economics book's eleven-entry list.</p>
    <p><b>"The round glowing mechanism must be a button."</b> It responds once, early, and never again. It is not the safe and not the keypad. Do not build a theory around it.</p>

    <h2>Where Study Room sits in Chapter 3<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>Chapter 3 is <i>Murder Dressed in White</i>. Study Room is room <b>3 of 15</b>, between Living Room and Guest Toilet, and it is the chapter's device level: two of its three codes exist only on screens. That is worth knowing before you start, because it changes where you look — the laptop and the phone are locks and clue-holders, not scenery.</p>
    <p>It is also the longest chain in the chapter's opening run. Guest Toilet after it has a single safe and a single key; Study Room has a password, a second factor, a derived code and an optional rotary safe. If you are working through in order, do the laptop before you touch anything else — the verification step is a wall you cannot climb without the phone, and the phone is easier to find once you know you need it.</p>

